  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If the solutions of the equation \\(x^2+px+q=0\\) are the cubes of the solutions of the equation \\(x^2+mx+n=0,\\) then",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(p=m^3+3mn\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(p=m^3-3mn\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(p+q=m^3\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\left(\\frac mn\\right)^3=\\frac pq\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "none of these"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1977-p23.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let the roots of \\(x^2+mx+n\\) be \\(u,\\) \\(v.\\) Then \\(u+v=-m\\) and \\(uv=n,\\) while the roots of the first equation are \\(u^3,\\) \\(v^3.\\) Thus \\[ \\begin{aligned} -p&=u^3+v^3\\\\ &=(u+v)^3-3uv(u+v)\\\\ &=-m^3+3mn. \\end{aligned} \\] Therefore \\(p=m^3-3mn.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Call the roots of the second equation \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) and apply Vieta’s formulas",
        "Use \\(u^3+v^3=(u+v)^3-3uv(u+v)\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
